\begin{blocksection}
Gleam is a functional programming language. In computer science, functional programming is
a paradigm where programs are constructed by applying and composing functions, and func-
tion definitions are trees of expressions that map values to other values, rather than a sequence
of statements which update the running state of the program. Gleam is a statically-typed,
type-safe language. Type safety is the extent to which a programming language discourages or
prevents type errors. The behaviors classified as type errors by a given programming language
are usually those that result from attempts to perform operations on values that are not of the
appropriate data type, e.g. trying to add a string to an integer.
Type enforcement can be static (catching potential errors at compile time), dynamic (associating
type information with values at run-time and consulting them as needed to detect imminent
errors), or a combination of both. In the context of static type systems, type safety usually
involves a guarantee that the eventual value of any expression will be a legitimate member of
that expression’s static type.
When writing functions that operate on a given input, Gleam requires that you handle any
theoretically possible output case, even if you’ve potentially handled such a case earlier. For
instance, the function list.max (which behaves very similarly to Python’s max function) does
not return an integer directly, but a Result. This handles the case in which an empty list has
been passed into max: it returns Ok(value) or Error(Nil) regardless of whether you’ve
already handled the empty case earlier in your code, and are thus guaranteeing that it will
always be an Ok result.
When writing a case statement in Gleam, you must handle every theoretically possible case,
even if that case may not be possible in the context of your own code. For instance, say you
have a function that exclusively outputs even integers, and you use one of those integers n in a
case statement:

\begin{lstlisting}
case n \% 2 {
    0 -> //some code for the case in which n is even
    1 -> //some code for the case in which n is odd
    }
\end{lstlisting}
Gleam will require you to include the latter case, in which n % 2 == 1, regardless of whether
your integer n could possibly be odd.
\end{blocksection}